WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.858
<v Instructor>Okay, so let's get into</v>

2
00:00:01.858 --> 00:00:03.990
AWS Elastic Beanstalk.

3
00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.510
Beanstalk is going to be more on the developer exam,

4
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.940
but from a solutions architect professional exam,

5
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.670
you need to understand that it is a great platform

6
00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.060
to re-platform your application from on-premise

7
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.950
to the AWS cloud.

8
00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.800
So the idea is that it's a developer-centric view

9
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.020
of deploying an application on AWS,

10
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.340
and it's just a wrapper

11
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.740
around all the components that we know from before.

12
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.980
So EC2 instances, auto scaling groups, Elastic IP,

13
00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.500
Elastic Cloud balancers, RDS, et cetera.

14
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.560
But it's in one view and it's easy to make sense of it.

15
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.020
So we still have control over all the configuration

16
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.540
of each components, but Beanstalk gives us

17
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.730
some flexibility for deployment.

18
00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.320
Beanstalk is going to be free,

19
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.380
but you're going to pay for the underlying instances.

20
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.930
Okay, So Beanstalk has support for many platforms,

21
00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.550
and you have to remember some of them.

22
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.030
Go, Java, Java with Tomcat, and that is very important

23
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.130
because if you have a Tomcat application on premise

24
00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.050
and you want to migrate it to the cloud,

25
00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.907
Java with Tomcat is a great option on Beanstalk.

26
00:01:00.907 --> 00:01:04.770
.Net applications for Windows server, node.js, PHP, Python,

27
00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:06.210
Ruby, Packer.

28
00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.280
So as you can see, there's a bunch of support

29
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.140
for many, many different runtimes.

30
00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.450
And if you don't have a runtime that is supported

31
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.310
by Beanstalk, it is possible for you

32
00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.398
to use a single Docker container,

33
00:01:15.398 --> 00:01:19.050
or a multicontainer Docker, or a preconfigured Docker.

34
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.930
The idea is that if you can dockerize your application,

35
00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.600
then you can migrate it to Elastic Beanstalk.

36
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.070
And so the idea is that Beanstalk

37
00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.380
is going to be a perfect solution

38
00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.030
to re-platform your application

39
00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.410
from on-premise to the cloud.

40
00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.290
That means moving your runtime onto Beanstalk

41
00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:35.940
and have your application run

42
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.370
as a native AWS application

43
00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.890
without changing the code too much.

44
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.230
Okay, so Beanstalk is a managed service.

45
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.420
That means that all the instance configuration

46
00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.120
and the OS configuration will be handled by Beanstalk.

47
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.680
The deployment strategy is configurable,

48
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.390
but it will be performed by Beanstalk as well,

49
00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.730
so we have different rollout strategies.

50
00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.350
The only thing in responsibility

51
00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.780
of you is the application code.

52
00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.340
And so you have three architecture models

53
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.840
on Beanstalk we can see,

54
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.730
the number one is a single-instance deployment,

55
00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.350
which is great for dev.

56
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:06.600
Load balancer plus ASG, which is good for production

57
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.580
or pre-production web applications.

58
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.740
And finally, just an OTO scaling group,

59
00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.030
which is good if you have

60
00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.860
a non-web application in production,

61
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.440
and this is usually called a worker environment.

62
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.273
So let's have a look

63
00:02:17.273 --> 00:02:19.173
at how it looks like in terms of diagram.

64
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.460
So the first thing we have is going to be a single instance,

65
00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.550
and the architecture chosen by Beanstalk in this example

66
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:29.310
is obviously only one availability zone.

67
00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.140
We have an EC2 instance in it,

68
00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.830
and we'll have an elastic IP attached to our EC2 instance.

69
00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:38.460
Why? Because in case the EC2 instance is replaced

70
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:42.000
for whatever updates, then the elastic IP can be moved over

71
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.710
to the new EC2 instance,

72
00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.660
and from a client perspective,

73
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.900
the change in the upgrade is going to be seamless.

74
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.270
Finally, you can set up an Amazon RDS database

75
00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.790
through Beanstalk for having your EC2 instance

76
00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.250
communicate with a database layer if you needs to.

77
00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.890
And that RDS instance is going to be, obviously,

78
00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.780
set up in only one AZ.

79
00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.660
So, these single-instance deployments are great

80
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:04.890
for development environments

81
00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.200
because you can quickly show your application

82
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.600
and it's going to be very cost effective.

83
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.380
If you want high availability, a more production-like setup,

84
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.293
then you need to use a load balancer, for example, ALB.

85
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.070
And then your application is going to be multi-AZ.

86
00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.380
So in this example, I've only represented two AZ,

87
00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.940
but it can obviously be three.

88
00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.270
So you have two AZ, an auto-scaling group of EC2 instances

89
00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:30.570
that can scale over time to handle more or less traffic.

90
00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.720
And then we can even set up an RDS database, again,

91
00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.970
in terms of storing our backend data.

92
00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.370
But this time, because this is more a production deployment,

93
00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:41.580
we should use a multi-AZ deployment of RDS

94
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.060
where we have a master and a standby database.

95
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.470
That is for number two.

96
00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.230
And then number three is a worker tier,

97
00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:50.400
which is very important.

98
00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.980
So in this example, we have an SQS queue

99
00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.360
that is managed by Beanstalk,

100
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.040
and we have a worker tier

101
00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:59.190
which consists of an EC2 auto-scaling group

102
00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.350
that will read from the SQS queue

103
00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.590
and scale based on the queue load.

104
00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.540
So the idea is that we have three different kinds

105
00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:07.560
of deployment for Beanstalk

106
00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.050
and you need to know them going into the exam.

107
00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.900
Now let's talk about the difference between a web server

108
00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.360
and a worker environment in Beanstalk.

109
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.880
So in case your application performs long tasks,

110
00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.760
and this task can take a lot of CPU power

111
00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.360
and threads from your web servers, it's usually better

112
00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.530
to upload these tasks into a dedicated worker environment.

113
00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:30.780
And by doing this, you are decoupling your application

114
00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.820
into two tiers, and that is very common.

115
00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.710
And so anytime we hear decoupling,

116
00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.230
remember it has to be SQS.

117
00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:38.940
So for example, what are the kind of tasks

118
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.470
that could be long to complete?

119
00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.720
While processing a video if you want to add

120
00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.790
some kind of text on top of the video,

121
00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.320
or generating a zip file,

122
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.100
or creating a very complicated filter for your images,

123
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.300
et cetera.

124
00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.490
On top of it, in your worker environment

125
00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.250
you are able to define a bunch of periodic tasks

126
00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.650
to be done using a cron.yami file,

127
00:04:58.650 --> 00:04:59.730
which will run cron jobs

128
00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.400
directly from your worker environment.

129
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:03.233
Okay, so let's have a look

130
00:05:03.233 --> 00:05:06.150
at a typical architecture for Beanstalk.

131
00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.040
You would have your web tier for Beanstalk,

132
00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.250
which consists of your ALB and your auto scaling group,

133
00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.980
and that is going to be a production-like deployment.

134
00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.380
And then we're going to have a worker tier,

135
00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.770
also managed by Beanstalk, which consists of an SQS queue

136
00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:22.890
and an EC2 instances managed by an auto scaling group.

137
00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:27.210
And the idea is that if the web tier has some dedicated task

138
00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.660
that are going be long to complete, then it would insert,

139
00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.510
put these tasks into an SQS queue,

140
00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.070
and your auto scaling group would

141
00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:36.960
automatically process these tasks.

142
00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.520
This is a classic architecture,

143
00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.353
and the good thing is that

144
00:05:39.353 --> 00:05:42.960
both of these tiers can be managed using Beanstalk.

145
00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.540
Okay, next we have blue/green deployment,

146
00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.760
which is not a direct feature of Beanstalk,

147
00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.220
but the idea is that it gives you zero downtime

148
00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:51.750
and release facility.

149
00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.940
So the idea is that you would create a new stage environment

150
00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.590
and you would deploy a v2 there.

151
00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:58.170
So you create a new Beanstalk environment,

152
00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.910
then you validate that this environment,

153
00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:03.840
which is the green environment, can be working as expected,

154
00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.100
and in case there is any problem,

155
00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.170
you can roll it back by deleting it,

156
00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.510
and then you would set up Route 53

157
00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:10.950
to use weighted routing

158
00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.050
to make sure you can shift a little bit of traffic

159
00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:14.400
to the new environment.

160
00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.650
If you use the Beanstalk Swap URL feature,

161
00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:20.040
which is a DNS swap, this basically shifts all the traffic

162
00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.570
from one environment to another,

163
00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:23.490
so you have two different strategies.

164
00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.733
So here's the example, using weighted routing in Route 53.

165
00:06:27.646 --> 00:06:30.810
So the web traffic does DNS queries onto Route 53,

166
00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:32.130
and then maybe 90%

167
00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.910
of your traffic will be sent into your blue environment,

168
00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.920
and 10% of your traffic will be sent

169
00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.510
into your green environment.

170
00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:43.350
And that is using a weighted routing policy in Route 53.

171
00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.380
And then you can shift the percentages over time.

172
00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.660
And then when you're ready, you have effectively shifted

173
00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.330
all your traffic from your v1 to your v2.

174
00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:55.050
And this is called a blue/green deployment, okay?

175
00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:56.610
So that's all you need to know for Beanstalk

176
00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:57.630
going into the exam.

177
00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.820
Remember, Beanstalk is going to be a great platform

178
00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:02.760
when you want to migrate an application

179
00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.670
from on-premise to the cloud and re-platform it.

180
00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.100
And finally, it's going to be a great choice

181
00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:10.380
to manage both your web server applications

182
00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:11.850
and your worker tiers.

183
00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:13.900
Okay, I will see you in the next lecture.

